; Two banks and a middle, same shapes and the same 100 px gaps as Gauntlet's
; ground -- proven reach numbers (pickaxe hooks either lip at ~110 px, the
; axe and sword too, the dagger flings it), so the crossing itself is never
; the hard part here. What is new is what's blowing through it: issue #52's
; WindZone, the star of this stage.
;
; **The gale.** Two zones, one over each gap, each spilling 40 px onto the
; bank on its outer side and the middle on its inner side but never as far
; as a spawn point. Both blow the same way, RIGHT, so this is one prevailing
; wind rather than two local ones: crossing rightward (BankLeft->Middle,
; Middle->BankRight) rides the gust, crossing leftward fights it, and anyone
; parked on the downwind lip of a gap during a gust -- Middle's or BankLeft's
; east edge, over either gap -- gets shoved straight into it. That's the
; ring-out this stage is built around: not a cliff at the map's edge, but
; the two gaps everyone already has to cross, turned hostile on a timer. The
; upwind lip of each gap only ever gets blown back to safety, so which side
; of a gap is dangerous depends on which way you're facing, not where you
; are.
;
; The two zones are offset by half the 5 s cycle (2.5 s), so they never gust
; together: one gap is always either calm or just winding up while the other
; is live. A player can read the tell (the streaks brighten and speed up a
; full second before the push starts) and either wait it out, retreat to the
; other, calmer gap, or -- since heads are never pushed (see WindZone.gd) --
; plant a hook on solid ground and let the gust stretch the arm instead of
; the body. That hold applies wherever the head lands, inside the gust or
; out, so nobody is ever forced onto a specific "safe" tile to ride out a
; gust; they just have to be anchored to something.
;
; Overlook sits above the middle, out of both zones' reach (they stop 40 px
; short of it on either side) -- a windless perch and the one place a hook
; from Middle has to climb rather than cross: 142 px up, within the
; pickaxe's reach with a plant near the ledge's corner, and dead calm once
; you're on it. It is a refuge from the gale, not a route around it -- both
; gaps still have to be crossed to get anywhere else -- and it drops
; straight back onto Middle on either side, so nobody can get stuck up there
; once the floor starts rising.
;
; Reach math, same stage scale as Gauntlet: a body's centre sits 24 px above
; whatever it's standing on. Middle's top and both banks' tops are y=300, so
; every standing surface starts even, and the rising floor takes the whole
; stage together. The 100 px gaps are hookable from either lip by every
; weapon but the dagger, which has to fling it -- an easy fling at this
; width, gale or no gale.

; Backdrop (issue #117): storm.
